<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_single_obstacle_real">
  <!-- Real-table push past one pudding box standing on the straight line
       between block start and goal.

       Start (0.381, 0.343) -> goal (0.381, -0.305): the same x, so the direct
       route is a straight run along -y of |d| = 0.648 m. The obstacle sits on
       that line, a third of the way along it:
         along   = 1/3    ->  y = 0.343 - 0.648/3 = 0.127
         lateral = 0      ->  x = 0.381
       Nearer the start than the goal, deliberately: the block has to commit
       to a detour early rather than run most of the way and then swerve.

       This does NOT copy sim's single_obstacle layout, which was what this
       file used to do (68.9% along, 8.3% off to one side, giving the geom at
       (0.327, -0.103)). That placement leaves the direct route open, so the
       scene did not test what it is named for.

       It blocks the whole route, not part of it. Rotated 90 degrees the box
       is 0.089 m wide in x, spanning x in [0.3365, 0.4255]; the block's
       crossbar is 0.089 m wide too and spans exactly the same interval at the
       start pose. The block therefore cannot pass without moving sideways by
       its own full width.

       Clearances, all checked: the obstacle reaches y = 0.181 and the block's
       leading edge at the start pose is at y = 0.264, so they start 0.083 m
       apart; the table spans x in [-0.05, 0.713] and y in [-0.7615, 0.7615],
       so the box is well inside it.

       Keep in sync with SCENES["single_obstacle_real"] in oim/utils/scenes.py;
       tests/test_scenes.py checks the two agree. -->
  <include file="common_real.xml"/>
  <include file="tee_real.xml"/>
  <worldbody>
    <camera name="front" pos="1.4907 0.3750 0.8684" xyaxes="0.0000 1.0000 -0.0000 -0.4226 0.0000 0.9063"/>
    <!-- Mocap, matching box_clutter_real: pose is written once at run
         start from ArUco detection (oim.worlds.real3d), and pos/euler
         here are exactly the bare geom's old values, so a run without
         --obstacle-calibration is unchanged. A geom sitting directly in
         <worldbody> has its pose baked into the compiled model and
         cannot be repositioned at runtime; a mocap body carries its pose
         in mjData.mocap_pos/quat, which is what apply_obstacle_
         calibration writes. Leaving this as a bare geom made the two
         calibration appliers disagree -- the planner Box at
         SCENES["single_obstacle_real"].obstacles[0] would move to the
         detected pose while this geom stayed put. -->
    <body name="obs_1" mocap="true" pos="0.381 0.127 0.0298" euler="0 0 90">
      <geom class="obstacle" name="obs_1" type="box" size="0.054 0.0445 0.0298"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>